You're talking as though NetHack is "becoming" something, which is
both presumptuous and wrong.  NetHack has been stable (as in,
completely unchanged) for more years than most people have played it.
It has remained largely unchanged since the late 1980s when, as I used
to refer to it, "that fountain crap" was introduced into the game.  I
can't read your mind, but I strongly suspect that the change you
perceive is an evolution in *your* perception and understanding of the
game, because  the game itself is certainly not a moving target.

I really, *really* oppose the introduction of changes that increase
the number of fatal or game-wrecking mistakes that a missed keystroke
or single thinko yields.  That part of NetHack's balance is very
finely tuned - over the course of more than a decade - and is
fundamental to its character and audience.  You might as well write a
game where you are presented with 100,000 digits of pi and then
required to type them in correctly without hitting backspace in order
to win.  NetHack is already intolerant of mistakes, but it responds to
those mistakes in an offhand, lighthearted way.  When you die, it
tells you about it in an amusing way and reminds you that you are just
playing a game and that you are invited back into it to try again.  At
every point it wryly reminds you that it doesn't take itself
seriously.  At all.

Being able to get away with cheap tricks and "game the game" is also
fundamental to NetHack and its culture.  The best and most time-
honored example of this is that you can train pets to steal from
shops.  It is a metaphor for the game in its entirety.  NetHack
*wants* you to play outside the box.  It *wants* you to exploit its
loopholes.  NetHack's loopholes are *not* there by accident.

Do you think that anyone *accidentally* wrote pudding farming into the
game, or failed to understand its implications?

Before you (or anyone else) conclude that pudding farming, or any
other gimmick, is something that needs to be fixed, please do
understand that it was put there for a reason, and that it was put
there by very thoughtful people.



